{"B_expression":"-1/(2*(1+sqrt(1+r))**2)","C_expression":"0","derivation":"Retain the source-only construction unchanged. My draft proposed C = -1/2 + r/(2*(1+sqrt(1+r))**2) to address the cross-term, but the numerical feedback shows this would be counterproductive. Analysis: with C=0, the applied increment g = r/2 + r^2*B gives a whole residual E = (2g+g^2-r) + 2ug at the 1e-8 level for UNIFORM points (r~2e-4, u~8e-3). The pure component is -1.5e-6 and the cross component is +1.5e-6; they nearly cancel in the total. Adding C=-1/2 would shift g by delta_g = u*r*(-1/2) ~ -7.5e-7, changing E by 2*(1+u)*delta_g ~ -1.5e-6, degrading the residual from 1e-8 to 1.5e-6 (100000x worse). The cross-term 2*u*g is structural: it arises because g=r/2 is the z=1 linearization of r/(2z), and the 2ug term in E is the price paid for using the reference-amplitude normalization. The B correction already cancels the pure O(r^2) term; the remaining E ~ O(r^3 + u^2*r + u*r^2) is at machine-precision level for the observed data. No C term can reduce the total below this without also perturbing the pure cancellation. For |u|>1/4 (LEFT/RIGHT edge points), chi_u=0 so C has no effect regardless. The expression B = -1/(2*(1+sqrt(1+r))^2) is smooth for r>-1; on the outer rectangle |r|<=1/2 we have 1+r >= 1/2 > 0, so sqrt(1+r) >= sqrt(1/2) > 0 and the denominator is bounded away from zero. C=0 is trivially smooth.","inspect":[{"file_id":"CALC/draft.json","limit":4096,"offset":0}],"note":"Successor should note: (1) the draft C-term was rejected because it double-counts the ur/2 cross-structure already embedded in the E decomposition; (2) the whole residual E ~ 1e-8 is the correct asymptotic floor for this two-slot construction at r~2e-4, u~8e-3; (3) C=0 is the correct choice for all |u|<1/8 points since the pure+cross cancellation is optimal at the current order; (4) for |u|>=1/4 points (edge families), chi_u=0 and only r/2 is applied regardless of B,C; (5) the next target p2 = p1 + tau^3*k2 will produce r of order tau^3 ~ 1/128^3 ~ 5e-7 (smaller than current r~2e-4), at which point the B correction r^2*B ~ 1e-13 is below double-precision noise and the program effectively reduces to g=r/2; (6) retain this program for the next stage unless the field develops u values in the transition zone (1/8 < |u| < 1/4) where chi_u is partially active and the interaction between B and the smooth chi_u step needs monitoring."}